{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_53qn7ilqb6p6mkzgmvf7gqo5odipzznz4jft5fs7a5fjchfddrtq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass, with zero divergences found.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single 22-line translation unit exposing one symbol:\n\n```c\nvoid sieve(int val) {\n    while (1) { printf(\"%d\\n\", val); if (val % 10 == 9) break; val++; }\n}\n```\n\nIts only observable is the stdout byte stream, so every test compares raw bytes.\n\n## Phase A artifacts (`translation/`)\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — `nm -D` shows the C `.so` exports exactly one defined symbol, `sieve`. The Rust `.so` exports it under the identical name. **Diff C→Rust is empty**, and no whole C module was skipped (one `.c` file, no `#ifdef`s, no macro-generated symbols). Nothing needed stubbing or translating.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — grep proves the explicit error surface is *empty*: 0 `return`s, 0 asserts, 0 range/null checks, 0 enums, 0 min/max constants. So I enumerated the 12 real rejection-adjacent conditions instead (all-negative inputs, `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX`, the 8-value overflow region, hostile bit patterns, garbage in the upper argument register, closed stdout, …).\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 22 rows from the axes the code actually branches on: sign, `val % 10` class, `%d` width, mid-run decimal carry, `INT_MAX` proximity, call multiplicity, stdout kind, concurrency.\n\n## Phases B/C/D\n\n41 tests across `valid_paths.rs` (22), `error_paths.rs` (12), `symbols.rs` (6) — all green in **6 configurations** (default/`--no-default-features`/`--all-features` × debug/release, automated in `run_all_configs.sh`). Both libraries are only ever reached via `dlopen`/`dlsym`, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrapper is under test.\n\nNotable engineering the C forced:\n- Each measurement re-executes the test binary and `dup2`s a private file/FIFO onto fd 1 — fd 1 can't be safely hijacked inside multi-threaded libtest, and it keeps the two same-named `sieve` symbols unconfusable.\n- Rows whose loops run ~2³¹ times (`INT_MAX`, `INT_MIN`) are compared as **bounded 1 MiB prefixes**, killing the child once enough bytes land.\n\n## Two findings worth your attention\n\n1. **A harness bug that made the suite silently vacuous.** `cargo test` does *not* rebuild a `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` library, since integration tests can't link one. The suite was comparing C against a **stale** `.so` — a deliberately broken `lib.rs` passed all 41 tests. `rust_lib()` now rebuilds from current sources into a separate `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` and asserts the artifact is newer than its sources. I found this only because I mutation-tested the harness, so `mutation_check.sh` is now checked in: it injects 9 plausible mistranslations (`rem_euclid`, `saturating_add`, test-before-print, `%u`, CRLF, …) and **all 9 are caught**.\n\n2. **The C's negative-input behaviour contradicts its own comment.** The comment says it stops \"when the count ends in 9\", but C's truncating `%` means `-9 % 10 == -9`, so `sieve(-9)` does *not* stop — it counts up to `+9`. The Rust preserves this; `rem_euclid` would have \"fixed\" it and is caught by 11 tests. Likewise `sieve(INT_MAX)` relies on signed-overflow UB; I confirmed GCC 11.5 wraps at `-O0`/`-O2`/`-O3` alike, so `wrapping_add(1)` is correct however the C is built.\n\n**No divergence was found — `translation/src/lib.rs` required no behavioural changes.** The only edits were `libloading` in `[dev-dependencies]` plus new test/doc files; `c_src/` is unmodified apart from the `build/` directory the task asked me to create."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":551,"cache_read_input_tokens":116826,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":551,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2971ca4